<Project>

  <!--
    ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    VENDORED COPY. The canonical file is Broiler.Browser's eng/Broiler.Configurations.props,
    and this is one of the copies its own comments call "a vendored copy": a component that
    must decompose Debug-VM/Release-VM with no parent repository above it.

    Broiler.HtmlBridge is unusual in vendoring it AND chaining to the parent when there is
    one, because the -VM pair is this component's own pair rather than something a parent
    imposes. Both guards below are what keep the two runs to a single decomposition; read
    them before editing either copy.

    A few passages below describe the BROWSER's tree - which submodules chain, and
    src/Broiler.Browser.Core being the one site that reads $(BroilerJavaScriptEngine). They
    are left as written because they explain why the mechanism is shaped this way. What is
    true here: the property selects Broiler.HtmlBridge.Scripting.Vm and
    Broiler.HtmlBridge.Jseal.Vm, and it defines BROILER_VM_JS, which the VM-only test cases
    are guarded on.
    ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  -->

  <!--
    ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    Broiler build configurations
    ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

    The repository builds more configuration names than the two MSBuild knows
    about. Each one decomposes into a BASE configuration (Debug or Release,
    which decides symbols and optimisation) and one AXIS the name adds:

      Debug        Release            the base configurations
      Debug-Linux  Release-Linux      pin linux-x64 on the Linux head
      Debug-Windows Release-Windows   pin win-x64 on the Windows head
      Debug-VM     Release-VM         run script on the Broiler.VM JavaScript
                                      profile instead of Broiler.JS

    WITHOUT THIS MAPPING A NAMED CONFIGURATION SILENTLY BUILDS AS NEITHER. The
    SDK keys DebugSymbols off `Configuration == 'Debug'` and Optimize off
    `Configuration == 'Release'`, so `-c Release-VM` compiles UNOPTIMISED and
    `-c Debug-VM` compiles WITHOUT SYMBOLS — a build that succeeds and is wrong
    in a way nothing reports. Evaluating any project here proves it:

      dotnet msbuild <project> -p:Configuration=Release-VM -getProperty:Optimize

    answers empty until this file runs, and `true` after.

    ── Why this is a separate file ───────────────────────────────────────

    It is imported TWICE, from two places, because the mapping has to reach
    projects the root Directory.Build.props cannot:

      Directory.Build.props    — for every project that chains to the root.
      Directory.Build.targets  — for the ones that do not.

    FOUR submodules carry a Directory.Build.props that deliberately does not
    chain to a parent: Broiler.Input, Broiler.JS, Broiler.Media and Broiler.VM.
    (Broiler.VM's says so in a comment and gives the reason: chaining would
    inherit this repository's suppression list and silence diagnostics a new
    component has no legacy reason to suppress.) MSBuild stops at the first
    Directory.Build.props it finds, so the root's never runs for any of them —
    but Directory.Build.targets is looked up separately, and that is the
    asymmetry the second import exploits.

    Three of the four have no Directory.Build.targets at all, so the root's
    reaches them without their doing anything. Broiler.JS has one, and it used
    to stop the lookup dead; it now chains to its parent explicitly, so it
    arrives here by a third route to the same place. That is a file in that
    submodule rather than this one — docs/vm-javascript-profile.md says why.

    It matters now because Debug-VM/Release-VM are the first configurations
    whose project graph reaches INTO Broiler.VM. Release-Linux never did, so
    the gap cost nothing and stayed invisible.

    ── Overriding without switching configuration ────────────────────────

    $(BroilerJavaScriptEngine) is a property, not a literal test on the
    configuration name, so a developer can build any configuration against
    either engine:

      dotnet build ... -p:BroilerJavaScriptEngine=Vm

    ── What this file does NOT do ────────────────────────────────────────

    It makes the names BEHAVE. It does not make projects DECLARE them, which is a
    separate problem with a separate answer in eng/Broiler.ConfigurationNames.targets
    — separate because a project's own <Configurations> is evaluated after this and
    overwrites it, so the declaration has to run later than the mapping can.

    Leaving that undone did not break any build; it broke opening the solution in
    Visual Studio, which is the one reader that checks.
  -->

  <!--
    TWO GROUPS WITH TWO GUARDS, BECAUSE THERE ARE TWO WAYS TO ARRIVE HERE TWICE AND THEY DO NOT
    COVER THE SAME PROPERTIES.

    $(BroilerConfigurationMappingApplied) stops the props-side and targets-side imports in THIS
    repository from both firing for one project. It guards the first group.

    '$(BroilerBaseConfiguration)' == '' guards the second group and stops something else: four
    submodules — Broiler.Input,
    Broiler.JS, Broiler.Media and Broiler.UI — carry their own VENDORED COPY of this mapping,
    which runs at props time and knows the four -Linux/-Windows names. For those names the
    configuration is already decomposed by the time the targets-side import gets here, and
    re-decomposing it appended DEBUG or RELEASE and LINUX or WINDOWS a second time:

      Broiler.Input at Release-Windows  ;RELEASE;WINDOWS;TRACE;RELEASE;WINDOWS;RELEASE_WINDOWS

    A duplicate conditional symbol is harmless to the compiler, but a constants list that reads
    differently depending on which component you ask is a bad way to answer the question this
    file exists to make answerable. A vendored copy that does NOT know the configuration — every
    one of them at Debug-VM/Release-VM — leaves the property empty, so this still runs and is
    still what maps those names.

    Broiler.UI was for a while a duplicate neither condition could reach: it chains to the root
    props AND carries a copy, so both ran at props time, where the targets-side guard never gets
    a say. It now applies the same '$(BroilerBaseConfiguration)' == '' test to its own copy, which
    is the only side that could fix it.

    ── Why the engine selection is in a group of its own ──────────────────

    Only the DECOMPOSITION is skipped when a vendored copy already did it. The engine selection
    below is not, because no vendored copy has ever heard of it: putting it under the same guard
    made Broiler.Input, Broiler.JS and Broiler.Media answer $(BroilerJavaScriptEngine) as EMPTY at
    the four -Linux/-Windows names while every other component answered `BroilerJs`. Inert, since
    only src/Broiler.Browser.Core reads it — and exactly the "the answer depends on which
    component you ask" defect the guard above exists to remove, one property over.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(BroilerConfigurationMappingApplied)' != 'true'">
    <BroilerConfigurationMappingApplied>true</BroilerConfigurationMappingApplied>

    <!--
      WHICH JAVASCRIPT ENGINE THE BROWSER'S SCRIPT ENGINE IS. `BroilerJs` is the
      default and what every configuration outside the -VM pair builds.

      `Vm` does NOT remove Broiler.JS from the graph. The second half of that sentence
      used to be "and cannot", on the grounds that the profile's host-capability ABI is
      opaque bytes (VmBytes -> VmOpaqueRef) and a DOM cannot be projected through one.
      That argument is true about the capability channel and was never about the whole
      engine: the profile publishes an in-realm host surface a DOM-shaped embedder can
      use, and src/Broiler.HtmlBridge.Jseal.Vm is a JSEAL provider over it that declares
      JsCapabilities.Document. What keeps Broiler.JS in the graph is this repository's
      own seam — IDomBridgeRuntime.Attach takes a JSContext — and not the VM.

      What `Vm` selects is which IScriptEngine the browser instantiates — see
      docs/vm-javascript-profile.md for what that engine does and does not run on the VM,
      and docs/jseal.md for the provider.
    -->
    <BroilerJavaScriptEngine Condition="'$(BroilerJavaScriptEngine)' == '' And ('$(Configuration)' == 'Debug-VM' Or '$(Configuration)' == 'Release-VM')">Vm</BroilerJavaScriptEngine>
    <BroilerJavaScriptEngine Condition="'$(BroilerJavaScriptEngine)' == ''">BroilerJs</BroilerJavaScriptEngine>

    <!--
      The constant the ONE engine-selection site reads
      (src/Broiler.Browser.Core/BrowserApp.cs). It is defined from the property
      rather than from the configuration name so that -p:BroilerJavaScriptEngine=Vm
      reaches the source as well as the project graph; a switch that changed the
      references without changing the code would build the VM assemblies and never
      call them — and it lives beside the property rather than beside the other
      constants so that the two cannot disagree about which projects get it.
    -->
    <DefineConstants Condition="'$(BroilerJavaScriptEngine)' == 'Vm'">$(DefineConstants);BROILER_VM_JS</DefineConstants>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(BroilerBaseConfiguration)' == ''">
    <BroilerBaseConfiguration Condition="'$(Configuration)' == 'Debug-Linux' Or '$(Configuration)' == 'Debug-Windows' Or '$(Configuration)' == 'Debug-VM'">Debug</BroilerBaseConfiguration>
    <BroilerBaseConfiguration Condition="'$(Configuration)' == 'Release-Linux' Or '$(Configuration)' == 'Release-Windows' Or '$(Configuration)' == 'Release-VM'">Release</BroilerBaseConfiguration>
    <BroilerTargetOperatingSystem Condition="'$(Configuration)' == 'Debug-Linux' Or '$(Configuration)' == 'Release-Linux'">Linux</BroilerTargetOperatingSystem>
    <BroilerTargetOperatingSystem Condition="'$(Configuration)' == 'Debug-Windows' Or '$(Configuration)' == 'Release-Windows'">Windows</BroilerTargetOperatingSystem>

    <DebugSymbols Condition="'$(BroilerBaseConfiguration)' == 'Debug'">true</DebugSymbols>
    <DebugType Condition="'$(BroilerBaseConfiguration)' == 'Debug'">portable</DebugType>
    <Optimize Condition="'$(BroilerBaseConfiguration)' == 'Debug'">false</Optimize>
    <DefineConstants Condition="'$(BroilerBaseConfiguration)' == 'Debug'">$(DefineConstants);DEBUG</DefineConstants>
    <DebugType Condition="'$(BroilerBaseConfiguration)' == 'Release'">portable</DebugType>
    <Optimize Condition="'$(BroilerBaseConfiguration)' == 'Release'">true</Optimize>
    <DefineConstants Condition="'$(BroilerBaseConfiguration)' == 'Release'">$(DefineConstants);RELEASE</DefineConstants>

    <DefineConstants Condition="'$(BroilerTargetOperatingSystem)' == 'Linux'">$(DefineConstants);LINUX</DefineConstants>
    <DefineConstants Condition="'$(BroilerTargetOperatingSystem)' == 'Windows'">$(DefineConstants);WINDOWS</DefineConstants>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
